      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. This is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. Applying the Euclidean Algorithm to 11 and 100, we get:\n\\[ 100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1, \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0. \\]\n\nRewriting the first equation, we have:\n\\[ 1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11. \\]\n\nThis tells us that:\n\\[ -9 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}, \\]\nso the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\( -9 \\). Since we want a positive inverse, we convert \\( -9 \\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[ -9 + 100 = 91. \\]\n\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can solve the original congruence by multiplying both sides by 91:\n\\[ t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nCalculating \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[ 36 \\cdot 91 = 3276. \\]\n\nTo find the last two digits of 3276, we compute:\n\\[ 3276 \\mod 100 = 76. \\]\n\nTherefore, \\( t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\). Since \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, the unique solution is:\n\\[ t = 76. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{76}. \\]",
