      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to find \\( t \\) such that:\n\n\\[\n11 \\cdot t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nThis means that \\( 11t \\) must end in 36. To find \\( t \\), we can first express \\( t \\) in the form:\n\n\\[\nt = 10a + b \\quad \\text{where} \\quad 0 \\leq b \\leq 9 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 0 \\leq a \\leq 9\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\( t = 10a + b \\) into the congruence:\n\n\\[\n11(10a + b) \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\n\\[\n110a + 11b \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 110a \\) is always divisible by \\( 10 \\), it doesn't affect the last two digits, so we can ignore \\( 110a \\):\n\n\\[\n11b \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( b \\), we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. The multiplicative inverse \\( x \\) of 11 modulo 100 is a number such that:\n\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find \\( x \\). Applying the Euclidean Algorithm:\n\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\n\nThis shows that the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\( -9 \\), which is equivalent to \\( 91 \\) modulo 100 (since \\(-9 + 100 = 91\\)).\n\nNow, we can solve for \\( b \\):\n\n\\[\n11b \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\\[\nb \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\\[\nb \\equiv 3276 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\\[\nb \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( b \\) must be a digit (0 through 9), we have \\( b = 6 \\). Now, we need to find \\( a \\):\n\n\\[\n11a + 6 \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\\[\n11a \\equiv 30 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( a \\), we can divide both sides by 11. The inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91, so:\n\n\\[\na \\equiv 30 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\\[\na \\equiv 2730 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\\[\na \\equiv 30 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( a \\) must be a digit (0 through 9), we have \\( a = 3 \\).\n\nThus, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\nt = 10a + b = 10 \\cdot 3 + 6 = 36\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]",
