      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We are given the equation:\n\\[11 \\cdot t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the congruence in a more manageable form:\n\\[11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(t\\), we need to find the modular inverse of 11 modulo 100. The modular inverse \\(x\\) of 11 modulo 100 is a number such that:\n\\[11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. First, we apply the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 11 and 100:\n\\[100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\\]\n\\[11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0\\]\n\nThe gcd is 1, and we can express 1 as a combination of 11 and 100:\n\\[1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\\]\n\nThis equation tells us that:\n\\[1 \\cdot 11 \\equiv -9 \\cdot 11 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\\[11 \\cdot (-9) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\\]\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since we want a positive inverse, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[-9 + 100 = 91\\]\n\nSo, the modular inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now, we can solve for \\(t\\) by multiplying both sides of the original congruence by 91:\n\\[t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nNext, we compute \\(36 \\cdot 91\\):\n\\[36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\\]\n\nNow, we find the remainder when 3276 is divided by 100:\n\\[3276 \\div 100 = 32 \\text{ remainder } 76\\]\nSo,\n\\[3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is a two-digit positive integer, the unique solution for \\(t\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{76}\\]",
