    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to find \\( t \\) such that \\( 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. The multiplicative inverse of 11 is a number \\( x \\) such that \\( 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100} \\).\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start with the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 11 and 100:\n\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\n\nThe gcd is 1, and we can work backwards to express 1 as a combination of 11 and 100:\n\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is -9, but we need a positive integer, so we convert -9 to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can solve the congruence \\( 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\) by multiplying both sides by 91:\n\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nWe calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the last two digits of 3276, which is 76. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\nt \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, the only solution is \\( t = 76 \\).\n\nThus, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) is \\(\\boxed{76}\\).",
